          "secNumber": "2.2",
          "heading": "Lesson 6: Atomic bonding and mineral definition",
          "content": "### Geological concept\n\nMinerals are naturally occurring crystalline solids characterized by ordered atomic arrangements and definable chemical compositions within permitted ranges. Distinguish an observed feature from an inferred history. Identify the material, physical scale, and duration at issue. A useful explanation should predict an additional observation that was not already used to construct the hypothesis.\n\n### Physical and chemical mechanisms\n\nIonic, covalent, and other bonding interactions influence hardness, cleavage, electrical behavior, and stability; lattice geometry can vary within compositional families. Sketch the necessary starting conditions, pathway, and geological record. Ask whether transport, phase change, reaction, or deformation is involved and whether later processes could modify the predicted evidence.\n\n### Evidence and analysis\n\nCrystal form, cleavage planes, hardness tests, optical properties, and compositional analysis help identify mineral species. Record sample provenance, measurement scale, instrument or descriptive method, and uncertainty. Where an observation can support more than one process, look for independent evidence that can discriminate between alternatives.\n\n### Field notebook \u2014 a hypothetical example\n\n**Setting:** A pale crystal from a hand specimen scratches glass and has irregular fracture.\n\n**Competing explanations:** Quartz is a plausible identification, but some feldspars or weathered materials may pass a superficial hardness screen.\n\n**Discriminating investigation:** Look for cleavage, check relative density, and obtain optical or diffraction evidence if mineral identity materially influences a petrologic interpretation.\n\nImagine presenting this evidence to a skeptical colleague. Specify three possible outcomes of the proposed test: supporting one explanation, favoring the alternative, or leaving the question unresolved. State any assumption about exposure, sample alteration, or instrument behavior.\n\n### Limits of the interpretation\n\nA single hardness or color test is rarely diagnostic; solid solutions and impurities can produce overlap among otherwise distinct minerals. The observed pattern need not determine a unique history; spatial coverage and the timing of later alteration affect confidence. Name a competing history in the field report and make clear whether its rejection depends on evidence that is actually available.\n\nCombine non-destructive screening with appropriate analytical methods while documenting how much material can be altered or destroyed. Distinguish a classroom inference from an applied decision with health, safety, environmental, or financial consequences. Professional site assessment requires appropriate standards and qualified personnel; the models here are learning aids.\n\n### Worked problem 6\n\n**Fictional input data:** A hand specimen has dry mass 156 g and geometric volume 60 cm\u00b3. Estimate bulk density.\n\n**Given:** m=156, V=60. Use consistent units and define relevant geometry.\n\n**Steps:** Evaluate `m/V` with the values above; the result is **2.6 g/cm\u00b3**. Independent numeric verification inputs are supplied in `WORKED-CASES.json`.\n\n**Interpretation:** Assumes accurate volume and no trapped air. This is not a measured quantity from a real exposure or site. Ask how the result changes under a different geological assumption.\n\n### Practice\n\n1. How can two minerals with the same chemical formula exhibit distinct physical properties? State a process, an observable test, and a rival interpretation.\n2. Recalculate the worked problem after changing one input by 10%; state the unit and why the changed value matters.\n3. Build a four-column evidence matrix: direct observations, proposed events, alternative events, and unresolved measurements.\n4. Identify which practical decision would require an additional field measurement and explain why.\n\n**Knowledge check:** Explain which inference this lesson supports, which it does not, and the single most valuable next observation.",
