          "heading": "Lesson 7: Silicate structures and common rock formers",
          "content": "### Geological concept\n\nSilicon\u2013oxygen tetrahedra form isolated units, chains, sheets, and frameworks; associated cations create mineral diversity. Distinguish an observed feature from an inferred history. Identify the material, physical scale, and duration at issue. A useful explanation should predict an additional observation that was not already used to construct the hypothesis.\n\n### Physical and chemical mechanisms\n\nConnectivity governs bonding directions and helps explain cleavage behavior in olivine, pyroxene, amphibole, mica, feldspar, and quartz groups. Sketch the necessary starting conditions, pathway, and geological record. Ask whether transport, phase change, reaction, or deformation is involved and whether later processes could modify the predicted evidence.\n\n### Evidence and analysis\n\nHand-specimen textures, polarized-light microscopy, and X-ray diffraction reveal complementary aspects of mineral composition and structure. Record sample provenance, measurement scale, instrument or descriptive method, and uncertainty. Where an observation can support more than one process, look for independent evidence that can discriminate between alternatives.\n\n### Field notebook \u2014 a hypothetical example\n\n**Setting:** A coarse mafic specimen contains dark prismatic crystals and pale feldspar.\n\n**Competing explanations:** Pyroxene, amphibole, and altered ferromagnesian minerals may resemble one another at hand-sample scale.\n\n**Discriminating investigation:** Measure cleavage angles on fresh grains, review thin-section relief and pleochroism, and use chemistry where the identification remains ambiguous.\n\nImagine presenting this evidence to a skeptical colleague. Specify three possible outcomes of the proposed test: supporting one explanation, favoring the alternative, or leaving the question unresolved. State any assumption about exposure, sample alteration, or instrument behavior.\n\n### Limits of the interpretation\n\nQuartz can be mistaken for feldspar in altered samples, and cleavage may be hidden by small grains or weathered surfaces. The observed pattern need not determine a unique history; spatial coverage and the timing of later alteration affect confidence. Name a competing history in the field report and make clear whether its rejection depends on evidence that is actually available.\n\nUse a mineral identification key with confidence ratings rather than assigning a species solely from appearance. Distinguish a classroom inference from an applied decision with health, safety, environmental, or financial consequences. Professional site assessment requires appropriate standards and qualified personnel; the models here are learning aids.\n\n### Worked problem 7\n\n**Fictional input data:** A simple silica-oxygen building unit contains 12 silicon centers with four corner positions each. Count tetrahedral corner positions before any oxygen sharing.\n\n**Given:** Si=12. Use consistent units and define relevant geometry.\n\n**Steps:** Evaluate `Si*4` with the values above; the result is **48 positions**. Independent numeric verification inputs are supplied in `WORKED-CASES.json`.\n\n**Interpretation:** Shared oxygen positions are deliberately counted more than once. This is not a measured quantity from a real exposure or site. Ask how the result changes under a different geological assumption.\n\n### Practice\n\n1. Why do two cleavage directions in pyroxene not imply the same structural connectivity as amphibole? State a process, an observable test, and a rival interpretation.\n2. Recalculate the worked problem after changing one input by 10%; state the unit and why the changed value matters.\n3. Build a four-column evidence matrix: direct observations, proposed events, alternative events, and unresolved measurements.\n4. Identify which practical decision would require an additional field measurement and explain why.\n\n**Knowledge check:** Explain which inference this lesson supports, which it does not, and the single most valuable next observation.",
