          "heading": "Lesson 8: Mineral properties and identification",
          "content": "### Geological concept\n\nHardness, streak, luster, cleavage, fracture, density, and crystal habit are measurements or observations with distinct physical origins. Distinguish an observed feature from an inferred history. Identify the material, physical scale, and duration at issue. A useful explanation should predict an additional observation that was not already used to construct the hypothesis.\n\n### Physical and chemical mechanisms\n\nScratch tests compare relative resistance to abrasion, whereas cleavage indicates directions of weak bonds and density reflects mass per unit volume. Sketch the necessary starting conditions, pathway, and geological record. Ask whether transport, phase change, reaction, or deformation is involved and whether later processes could modify the predicted evidence.\n\n### Evidence and analysis\n\nHand-lens descriptions, streak plates, balance measurements, and controlled acid tests are standard screening observations when appropriate and safe. Record sample provenance, measurement scale, instrument or descriptive method, and uncertainty. Where an observation can support more than one process, look for independent evidence that can discriminate between alternatives.\n\n### Field notebook \u2014 a hypothetical example\n\n**Setting:** Two metallic-looking specimens show different streak colors despite similar luster.\n\n**Competing explanations:** The contrast may indicate different sulfides or oxides, but weathering coatings and mixed grains can distort both luster and streak.\n\n**Discriminating investigation:** Collect fresh surfaces using appropriate safety procedures and pair streak with density, magnetic response, and compositional results rather than one definitive scratch.\n\nImagine presenting this evidence to a skeptical colleague. Specify three possible outcomes of the proposed test: supporting one explanation, favoring the alternative, or leaving the question unresolved. State any assumption about exposure, sample alteration, or instrument behavior.\n\n### Limits of the interpretation\n\nSurface coatings, tiny grains, powder mixtures, and instrument calibration can distort classifications. The observed pattern need not determine a unique history; spatial coverage and the timing of later alteration affect confidence. Name a competing history in the field report and make clear whether its rejection depends on evidence that is actually available.\n\nPrefer several independent properties and record test conditions, sample alteration, and any irrecoverable uncertainty. Distinguish a classroom inference from an applied decision with health, safety, environmental, or financial consequences. Professional site assessment requires appropriate standards and qualified personnel; the models here are learning aids.\n\n### Worked problem 8\n\n**Fictional input data:** A mineral sample has mass 92 g and displaces 34 cm\u00b3. Estimate density as a screening measurement.\n\n**Given:** m=92, V=34. Use consistent units and define relevant geometry.\n\n**Steps:** Evaluate `m/V` with the values above; the result is **2.70588 g/cm\u00b3**. Independent numeric verification inputs are supplied in `WORKED-CASES.json`.\n\n**Interpretation:** Open pore and water absorption effects are ignored. This is not a measured quantity from a real exposure or site. Ask how the result changes under a different geological assumption.\n\n### Practice\n\n1. How would you distinguish a metallic sulfide from a superficially similar iron oxide using multiple tests? State a process, an observable test, and a rival interpretation.\n2. Recalculate the worked problem after changing one input by 10%; state the unit and why the changed value matters.\n3. Build a four-column evidence matrix: direct observations, proposed events, alternative events, and unresolved measurements.\n4. Identify which practical decision would require an additional field measurement and explain why.\n\n**Knowledge check:** Explain which inference this lesson supports, which it does not, and the single most valuable next observation.",
