          "secNumber": "4.4",
          "heading": "Lesson 18: Stratigraphic correlation and missing time",
          "content": "### Geological concept\n\nSuperposition, original horizontality, cross-cutting relationships, and fossil succession establish relative sequences under stated structural assumptions. Distinguish an observed feature from an inferred history. Identify the material, physical scale, and duration at issue. A useful explanation should predict an additional observation that was not already used to construct the hypothesis.\n\n### Physical and chemical mechanisms\n\nErosion, nondeposition, faulting, and facies changes produce incomplete records; an unconformity documents a hiatus, not necessarily its exact duration. Sketch the necessary starting conditions, pathway, and geological record. Ask whether transport, phase change, reaction, or deformation is involved and whether later processes could modify the predicted evidence.\n\n### Evidence and analysis\n\nMeasured sections, key beds, fossil ranges, magnetostratigraphy, and age constraints establish correlations. Record sample provenance, measurement scale, instrument or descriptive method, and uncertainty. Where an observation can support more than one process, look for independent evidence that can discriminate between alternatives.\n\n### Field notebook \u2014 a hypothetical example\n\n**Setting:** Two hills expose look-alike sandstone bodies separated by a covered valley.\n\n**Competing explanations:** Treating the bodies as the same age would assume away possible channel migration, fault repetition, and diachronous facies.\n\n**Discriminating investigation:** Search for a marker ash or fossil assemblage, map bedding attitudes and faults, and state the correlation confidence on the cross section.\n\nImagine presenting this evidence to a skeptical colleague. Specify three possible outcomes of the proposed test: supporting one explanation, favoring the alternative, or leaving the question unresolved. State any assumption about exposure, sample alteration, or instrument behavior.\n\n### Limits of the interpretation\n\nA matching sandstone appearance does not prove simultaneous deposition; diachronous boundaries can mislead. The observed pattern need not determine a unique history; spatial coverage and the timing of later alteration affect confidence. Name a competing history in the field report and make clear whether its rejection depends on evidence that is actually available.\n\nMaintain explicit uncertainty bands for correlations and identify what additional dated material would resolve ambiguity. Distinguish a classroom inference from an applied decision with health, safety, environmental, or financial consequences. Professional site assessment requires appropriate standards and qualified personnel; the models here are learning aids.\n\n### Worked problem 18\n\n**Fictional input data:** An 80-m sedimentary package accumulated over 2.5 million years. Find mean uncompacted thickness rate.\n\n**Given:** H=80, t=2.5. Use consistent units and define relevant geometry.\n\n**Steps:** Evaluate `H/t` with the values above; the result is **32 m/Ma**. Independent numeric verification inputs are supplied in `WORKED-CASES.json`.\n\n**Interpretation:** No compaction correction or erosional gaps. This is not a measured quantity from a real exposure or site. Ask how the result changes under a different geological assumption.\n\n### Practice\n\n1. How can a single volcanic ash bed support a time correlation that rock color cannot? State a process, an observable test, and a rival interpretation.\n2. Recalculate the worked problem after changing one input by 10%; state the unit and why the changed value matters.\n3. Build a four-column evidence matrix: direct observations, proposed events, alternative events, and unresolved measurements.\n4. Identify which practical decision would require an additional field measurement and explain why.\n\n**Knowledge check:** Explain which inference this lesson supports, which it does not, and the single most valuable next observation.",
