          "secNumber": "4.5",
          "heading": "Lesson 19: Radiometric dating and age interpretations",
          "content": "### Geological concept\n\nRadioactive parent isotopes decay with characteristic constants; measured parent\u2013daughter ratios yield ages only under appropriate system assumptions. Distinguish an observed feature from an inferred history. Identify the material, physical scale, and duration at issue. A useful explanation should predict an additional observation that was not already used to construct the hypothesis.\n\n### Physical and chemical mechanisms\n\nAn ideal closed system with known initial conditions permits a mathematical age estimate, but inheritance and later resetting complicate rock histories. Sketch the necessary starting conditions, pathway, and geological record. Ask whether transport, phase change, reaction, or deformation is involved and whether later processes could modify the predicted evidence.\n\n### Evidence and analysis\n\nMineral separation, analytical standards, concordance tests, isochrons, and field context help assess date reliability. Record sample provenance, measurement scale, instrument or descriptive method, and uncertainty. Where an observation can support more than one process, look for independent evidence that can discriminate between alternatives.\n\n### Field notebook \u2014 a hypothetical example\n\n**Setting:** A detrital mineral has a much older isotopic age than a bed's fossil assemblage.\n\n**Competing explanations:** The age can be consistent if the grain crystallized in older source rock before being eroded and redeposited.\n\n**Discriminating investigation:** Examine grain texture and inherited cores; obtain younger maximum depositional age constraints rather than reporting its crystallization date as basin deposition.\n\nImagine presenting this evidence to a skeptical colleague. Specify three possible outcomes of the proposed test: supporting one explanation, favoring the alternative, or leaving the question unresolved. State any assumption about exposure, sample alteration, or instrument behavior.\n\n### Limits of the interpretation\n\nA crystallization age, cooling age, metamorphic age, and maximum depositional age refer to different events. The observed pattern need not determine a unique history; spatial coverage and the timing of later alteration affect confidence. Name a competing history in the field report and make clear whether its rejection depends on evidence that is actually available.\n\nWrite the dated event explicitly beside any numerical age and test whether geologic relationships are consistent with it. Distinguish a classroom inference from an applied decision with health, safety, environmental, or financial consequences. Professional site assessment requires appropriate standards and qualified personnel; the models here are learning aids.\n\n### Worked problem 19\n\n**Fictional input data:** A parent isotope ratio remaining is 1/8 after decay. With half-life 0.70 Ga, find elapsed time.\n\n**Given:** f=0.125, h=0.7. Use consistent units and define relevant geometry.\n\n**Steps:** Evaluate `math.log(1/f,2)*h` with the values above; the result is **2.1 Ga**. Independent numeric verification inputs are supplied in `WORKED-CASES.json`.\n\n**Interpretation:** Initial parent known and closed system assumed. This is not a measured quantity from a real exposure or site. Ask how the result changes under a different geological assumption.\n\n### Practice\n\n1. Why does a detrital zircon age ordinarily not equal the time its sedimentary host layer was deposited? State a process, an observable test, and a rival interpretation.\n2. Recalculate the worked problem after changing one input by 10%; state the unit and why the changed value matters.\n3. Build a four-column evidence matrix: direct observations, proposed events, alternative events, and unresolved measurements.\n4. Identify which practical decision would require an additional field measurement and explain why.\n\n**Knowledge check:** Explain which inference this lesson supports, which it does not, and the single most valuable next observation.",
