          "secNumber": "4.6",
          "heading": "Lesson 20: Reading sedimentary basins",
          "content": "### Geological concept\n\nSubsidence, sediment supply, sea-level variation, and tectonics control the creation and filling of basins over geologic time. Distinguish an observed feature from an inferred history. Identify the material, physical scale, and duration at issue. A useful explanation should predict an additional observation that was not already used to construct the hypothesis.\n\n### Physical and chemical mechanisms\n\nAccommodation allows sediment accumulation, while compaction reduces porosity and changes observed thickness; later deformation may invert a basin. Sketch the necessary starting conditions, pathway, and geological record. Ask whether transport, phase change, reaction, or deformation is involved and whether later processes could modify the predicted evidence.\n\n### Evidence and analysis\n\nThickness trends, fossils, seismic reflection profiles, well logs, and sequence boundaries help reconstruct basin evolution. Record sample provenance, measurement scale, instrument or descriptive method, and uncertainty. Where an observation can support more than one process, look for independent evidence that can discriminate between alternatives.\n\n### Field notebook \u2014 a hypothetical example\n\n**Setting:** Porosity falls downward in a clay-rich basin succession.\n\n**Competing explanations:** Compaction is a candidate cause, but cementation, lithology change, and diagenesis may also change the measured void volume.\n\n**Discriminating investigation:** Compare grain packing, pore morphology, mineral cement and depth trends in equivalent facies before using porosity alone to infer loading history.\n\nImagine presenting this evidence to a skeptical colleague. Specify three possible outcomes of the proposed test: supporting one explanation, favoring the alternative, or leaving the question unresolved. State any assumption about exposure, sample alteration, or instrument behavior.\n\n### Limits of the interpretation\n\nPreserved basin fill is not a complete sediment input record; missing intervals and compaction change interpretation. The observed pattern need not determine a unique history; spatial coverage and the timing of later alteration affect confidence. Name a competing history in the field report and make clear whether its rejection depends on evidence that is actually available.\n\nUse measured thickness with sensitivity ranges before inferring ancient sedimentation rates or subsidence histories. Distinguish a classroom inference from an applied decision with health, safety, environmental, or financial consequences. Professional site assessment requires appropriate standards and qualified personnel; the models here are learning aids.\n\n### Worked problem 20\n\n**Fictional input data:** A sediment has void volume 0.24 m\u00b3 and total volume 0.80 m\u00b3. Compute porosity percentage.\n\n**Given:** Vp=0.24, Vt=0.8. Use consistent units and define relevant geometry.\n\n**Steps:** Evaluate `100*Vp/Vt` with the values above; the result is **30 %**. Independent numeric verification inputs are supplied in `WORKED-CASES.json`.\n\n**Interpretation:** All measured void space is included. This is not a measured quantity from a real exposure or site. Ask how the result changes under a different geological assumption.\n\n### Practice\n\n1. How could increasing deposit thickness reflect faster subsidence instead of faster sediment supply? State a process, an observable test, and a rival interpretation.\n2. Recalculate the worked problem after changing one input by 10%; state the unit and why the changed value matters.\n3. Build a four-column evidence matrix: direct observations, proposed events, alternative events, and unresolved measurements.\n4. Identify which practical decision would require an additional field measurement and explain why.\n\n**Knowledge check:** Explain which inference this lesson supports, which it does not, and the single most valuable next observation.",
